<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hussite Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1419">1419</a>–<a href="/years/1434">1434</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Hussite Revolution · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hussite Wars were fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from 1419 to about 1434 between the followers of the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a> and Catholic rulers led by Emperor Sigismund. Hussite armies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-zizka">Jan Žižka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prokop-the-great">Prokop the Great</a>, using wagon forts and firearms, defeated five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> sent against them; the moderate Utraquists then defeated the radical Taborites at Lipany (1434) and reached a settlement with the Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hussite-wars.jpg" alt="Hussite Wars" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hussitenkriege.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>From about 1402 the priest and scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a> denounced what he judged the corruption of the church and promoted some reformist ideas of the English theologian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wycliffe">John Wycliffe</a>. His preaching was widely heeded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. In 1414 Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> convened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a>, and Hus went there under a safe-conduct from Sigismund but was imprisoned, tried and executed on 6 July 1415.</p><p>The knights and nobles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> sent the protestatio Bohemorum to the Council on 2 September 1415, condemning the execution in the strongest language. Sigismund, persuaded that Hus was a heretic, threatened to drown all Wycliffites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a>, which greatly incensed the people. The death of his brother King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-iv-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus IV of Bohemia</a> then brought renewed unrest, and many Catholics, mostly Germans, were expelled from the Bohemian cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> was severe, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-zizka">Jan Žižka</a> left the city and defeated the Catholics at the Battle of Sudoměř on 25 March 1420, the first pitched battle of the wars. He then founded the Hussite settlement of Tábor. Pope Martin V proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> on 17 March 1420, and Sigismund and many German princes arrived before Prague on 30 June with a large army, but the siege was soon abandoned. The united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> set out their demands in the Four Articles of Prague.</p><p>Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> also failed. A second, in 1421, retreated from Žatec, and Žižka decisively defeated Sigismund at Německý Brod on 6 January 1422. After Žižka's death in October 1424 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prokop-the-great">Prokop the Great</a> took command of the Taborites. A crusade in 1427 under Cardinal Henry Beaufort was defeated at Tachov, and on 14 August 1431 a fifth crusade was routed at Domažlice.</p><p>Hussite militias, mostly commoners including women, relied on converted farm equipment and early firearms, and Žižka used transport carriages as wagon forts against cavalry charges. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prokop-the-great">Prokop the Great</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> also made raids called Spanilé jízdy, or glorious rides, into Silesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a> invited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> to negotiate in October 1431. In 1434 the Utraquists and Taborites went to war, and on 30 May the Taborite army was almost annihilated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lipany">Battle of Lipany</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prokop-the-great">Prokop the Great</a> and Prokop the Lesser both fell. The moderates then agreed the compacts, based on the Four Articles, which allowed communion in both kinds. Sigismund, the Hussite delegates and the Church's representatives signed them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihlava">Jihlava</a> on 5 July 1436.</p><p>The wars left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> ravaged. According to some estimates, the population of the Czech lands, put at 2.80 to 3.37 million around 1400, fell to 1.50 to 1.85 million by 1526. The adjacent Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> was left so impoverished that its effects were still evident in 1476.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hussite Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bohemia’s most original military achievement came in the <b>Hussite Wars</b> of 1419–1434.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Hussite Wars</b> of 1419–1434 most Moravian royal towns and many lords stayed loyal to Sigismund, and Hussite armies devastated parts of the land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hussites were a Czech proto-Protestant movement that followed Jan Hus after his execution in 1415 and fought the <b>Hussite Wars</b> of 1420 to 1434.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hussites">Hussites · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The battle virtually ended the <b>Hussite Wars</b> and opened the way to the Compacts of Basel.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-30">30 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
